    "---\n",
    "title: \"Stylometric Analysis of 19th Century French Novels with Spark NLP\"\n",
    "author: \"Luxuan HUANG\"\n",
    "format: html\n",
    "engine: jupyter\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# 1. Data Description and Extraction Pipeline\n",
    "\n",
    "## 1.1 Corpus Composition\n",
    "\n",
    "## 1.1 Corpus Composition\n",
    "\n",
    "The corpus consists of **29 French novels** from two literary movements:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Realist** – 19 novels by Émile Zola, most of them belonging to the *Rougon‑Macquart* cycle.\n",
    "- **Romantic** – 10 works by Victor Hugo, including major novels (e.g., *Notre‑Dame de Paris*, *Les Misérables*) as well as shorter pieces such as *Bug‑Jargal* and *Claude Gueux*.\n",
    "\n",
    "All texts were retrieved from **Project Gutenberg** in UTF‑8 plain text format. \n",
    "\n",
    "##1.2 Extraction Logic\n",
    "\n",
    "The goal of the extraction is to obtain only the narrative body of each novel, stripping away Gutenberg’s header, table of contents, and legal notices. This is achieved by:\n",
    "\n",
    "```         \n",
    "Checking if the cleaned file already exists in book_data/ (to avoid re‑downloading).\n",
    "\n",
    "Downloading the raw UTF‑8 file from Project Gutenberg once per unique URL (several short Hugo texts share the same URL).\n",
    "\n",
    "Extracting the text between the custom start and end tokens defined above.\n",
    "\n",
    "Saving the cleaned excerpt as book_data/<book_title>.txt.\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "The extraction pipeline is fully reproducible; running the code below on any machine with internet access will recreate the identical book_data/ directory.\n",
    "\n",
    "After execution, the book_data/ folder contains 29 cleaned .txt files, one per novel.\n",
    "\n",
    "#2. Load and Transform Pipeline\n",
    "\n",
    "The cleaned texts must be converted into a Spark DataFrame suitable for annotation. Given the **4 GB RAM constraint**, each novel is split into **chunks of 3000 words**. This keeps individual rows small enough to be processed by the NLP pipeline without triggering out‑of‑memory errors.\n",
    "\n",
    "The loading step creates a DataFrame with two columns: `book` (the short title) and `text` (a chunk).\n",
    "\n",
    "This simple whitespace‑based split preserves the raw surface form of the text, which is exactly what the NLP pipeline expects. The chunk size of 3000 was chosen empirically to keep individual rows small enough to avoid out‑of‑memory errors, while still providing enough context for the sentence detector and part‑of‑speech tagger to work accurately.\n",
    "\n",
    "No separate “load all books” DataFrame is created. Instead, loading, chunking, and annotation are performed book‑by‑book – an approach that fits both our memory constraints and the embarrassingly parallel nature of the task.\n",
    "\n",
    "# 3. Annotation Pipeline\n",
    "\n",
    "Annotation is performed with Spark NLP’s pretrained pipeline \"explain_document_ml\". This model provides:\n",
    "Sentence detection\n",
    "Tokenization\n",
    "Lemmatization\n",
    "Part‑of‑speech tagging\n",
    "\n",
    "It is multilingual (hence the ml suffix) and works out of the box for French.\n",
    "\n",
    "We launch Spark through sparknlp.start() with a heap limit of 1 GB and set spark.sql.shuffle.partitions to 1 to minimize shuffle overhead on a single‑machine setup. For each novel we:\n",
    "Read the cleaned text from book_data/.\n",
    "Split it into 3000‑word chunks.\n",
    "Create a small DataFrame with one column text.\n",
    "Apply the pretrained pipeline.\n",
    "Write the resulting annotated DataFrame to Parquet (overwrite mode).\n",
    "Discard the data and move to the next book.\n"
   ]
